[Music]  this weekend the country mourns together  as we mark the 21st anniversary of the 9  11 terror attacks the 9 11 memorial and  museum here in new york displays the  faces names and stories of the nearly 3  000 victims but two photos of the fallen  were missing adriana diaz has a story of  how a museum employee there made it his  personal mission to track down one of  the missing faces discovering the life  of a man we lost too soon in the tragedy  where the towers once stood  there's a constant flow of sorrow the  names of those lost here lined the  water's edge while just below ground  inside the 911 memorial and museum you  can look into their eyes i mean  everybody's face has a story behind it  says betty young and who made the famous  call  to the ground reporting the hijacking  underway jan seidla ramirez is the  museum's chief curator she's overseeing  the massive collection of these images  supplied by loved ones who volunteered  photos it sets up immediately the  diversity of the victims the magnitude  when people are in the gallery they will  go to the wall and they will find faces  that intrigue them what intrigued grant  yara who works in visitor services  helping guests was this missing photo  for albert ogletree one of two images  unaccounted for  he then made a promise to his friend in  security i pointed out the leaf and i  said to linda i said linda i'm going to  get the photo on the wall but little was  known about ogletree except that he  worked in a cafeteria in the north tower  his wife died after the attacks and he  had no known descendants  then on his own time yara found an  online tribute from a step-daughter he  tracked her down on facebook she did  write back to me however she did not  have any photos because  he did not like having his photo taken  he was camera shy he was people  associated with forte foods who were  food handlers they didn't have  the backing of an employer like the new  york city police department that had all  the different ways people  are photographed as part of a community  or as part of their employment so the  trail had run dry or did it  so i turned to ancestry.com and i was  able to find an address for him living  in romulus michigan i felt that maybe if  i could find where he went to high  school that maybe they would have a  photo of him this was really a shot in  the dark yes it was he emailed the high  school in romulus outside detroit they  didn't have older yearbooks but city  councilwoman and former teacher kathy  abdo who the school called for help knew  where to find them the local historical  museum i had an idea of what decade he  was in romulus so i went over and i went  through all of the yearbooks in the 60s  so i had to go through  page by page and that's where i found  albert ogletree the image is pixelated  but the camera shy ogletree is finally  found his only known photo taken around  15 years old this is someone that used  to go to my high school  and  he's not going to be forgotten now i'd  say i probably jumped a foot  you jumped a foot in the air yeah it was  a true  accomplishment  for albert for his memory to physically  hold it and see this sort of tender  sweet young face that you know the  adult life had not etched yet the really  wonderful moment was when the  step-daughter looked at the portrait and  she said oh my gosh he hasn't changed at  all we spoke to albert's step-daughter  and she said he taught me how to ride a  bike he helped teach me how to tie my  shoes she didn't want to appear on  camera but i can only imagine that this  is going to provide her with some kind  of closure  absolutely  the wide eyes and slight smile of a  young albert ogletree now appear above  his name  the details of his life are still  unclear but his face is in focus when  albert's photo went up i'm like linda  it's here i was like  wonderful  wonderful a promise made to a man from  the past he never knew  that he would never be  for cbs saturday morning adriana diaz  new york  that's taking those words never be  forgotten and actually putting it into  action yeah what a great story it really  was it really was and and her reaction  especially when yes and all of those  people are owning a piece of we've got  to find it a stranger a strong company  right

